// Gama-G core: the lifecycle of a secret.
//
// Spec section 8 gives sensitive data "stronger lifecycle controls, including
// restrictions on logging, serialization, and accidental conversion to ordinary
// Text", and section 12 says secrets cannot be printed through ordinary logging.
// Both are enforced here rather than advised. The binding is marked at the point
// it enters; an ordinary renderer refuses it; and letting the value out takes
// `secrets.expose` under the `SecretExpose` capability, which the intent has to
// declare and the audit log then records.
//
//   ggc run examples/core/custody.gg
//   ggc run examples/core/custody.gg --audit /tmp/custody.jsonl

gama core 0.2

intent Custody
    purpose    hold one patient identifier and disclose it only on purpose
    authority  SecretExpose, AuditWrite
    trail      custody of the identifier

source secret patientId : I64 from 4471

// A fingerprint is derived *from* the identifier without being the identifier,
// so it can leave. This is the ordinary way to use a secret: compute with it,
// disclose something that is not it.
operation Digest
    uses     patientId
    yields   fingerprint : Text
    effect   crypto
    computes secrets.fingerprint(patientId)
    trail    a fingerprint, not the identifier

// Disclosing the value itself is a different act, and the language treats it as
// one: it needs `SecretExpose`, it needs a reason, and it is audited.
operation Disclosed
    uses     patientId
    yields   revealed : Text
    effect   crypto, audit
    needs    SecretExpose
    computes to_text(secrets.expose(patientId, "clinician opened the record"))
    trail    deliberate disclosure

// The outcome is not the secret. Had it been, the generated entry point would
// have printed `[secret revealed]` instead of the value, because a program that
// wants a secret on stdout should say so in its own body.
outcome revealed
